; `(stack a b …)` layers its children so they all sound at once — the
; only combinator that produces overlapping haps, and the last of the
; seven never to have crossed the `sjon_query_pattern` boundary.
;
; Two `pure` atoms each span a whole cycle, so over the two-cycle window
; below there are four haps in two overlapping pairs. The ordering is the
; part a fixture pins that a unit test would not: the output is
; child-major, not time-sorted. Both of bd's cycles come first, then both
; of sn's, so [0 720720] appears at index 0 and again at index 2. A
; consumer that assumes the hap stream arrives in time order — a
; scheduler feeding an audio callback, say — is wrong about this, and
; the corpus compares haps in order, so any host that "helpfully" sorts
; them fails here.
(stack bd sn)
